# A crash dump is not an artifact: `core` was 1.3 GB of one here. `coscli` writes
# its log and output beside the checkout, and both are run output, not source.
core
coscli.log
coscli_output/

# Command Code's own state. The shared halves of it — `.commandcode/agents/` and
# `.commandcode/skills/` — are tracked, because they mirror `.claude/agents/` and
# `.claude/skills/`, which this repo already tracks. These three are not shared:
# `settings.json` holds machine-specific permission allows (absolute paths,
# one-off commands), `taste/` is rewritten by the preference learner, and
# `import-report.md` is a one-off migration report.
.commandcode/settings.json
.commandcode/import-report.md
.commandcode/taste/

# Screenshots stay local, the whole tree. They are observation records, not
# source: they go stale the moment a UI moves, they are megabytes of binary per
# capture session, and the capture runs leave scratch beside them — under
# `screenshots/ontplay/` that scratch included login scripts with a hardcoded
# password and an `auth-state.json` holding a live session token, which is the
# kind of thing a directory-level rule is for. Ignoring the tree outright means a
# capture run cannot sweep any of it into a commit by accident.
#
# This deletes 12 images upstream tracks (`PAP-*.png`, `PR-*.png`) — a deliberate
# divergence, recorded in `scripts/fork-surface.json`: at a merge, keep this rule
# and drop upstream's images again rather than restoring them.
screenshots/

# Replay evidence (agent-device screenshots) — local only. Per fork policy,
# capture artifacts (binary screenshots, .gitkeep only) never enter commits.
clients/*/replays/evidence/
**/replays/evidence/
